Kernel TYPEP does not recognise a closure as FUNCTION (CLOS dispatch misses it)
==============================================================================

SYMPTOM
-------
    (let ((clo (let ((n 5)) (lambda (x) (+ x n)))))
      (list (functionp clo)                       ; => T
            (typep clo 'function)                 ; => T (Lisp TYPEP)
            (funcall *%kernel-typep* clo 'function))) ; => NIL  <-- inconsistent

    (defgeneric g (x) (:method ((x function)) :fn) (:method (x) :other))
    (g (lambda () 1))   ; => :OTHER   (should be :FN)
    (g #'car)           ; => :FN      (a named/built-in function dispatches)

FUNCTIONP is true of a closure but the KERNEL TYPEP says it is not a FUNCTION,
so a CLOS method specialised on ((x function)) is not applicable to a closure
(CLOS %APPLICABLE-P tests built-in specializers via the kernel TYPEP, through
%TYPEP-BUILTIN).  Named / built-in functions dispatch fine; only closures are
missed — the closure's class is not seen as a subclass of FUNCTION.

Consequence: a blocker for bug-cl-ppcre — cl-ppcre's SCAN is a generic function
with a ((scanner function)) method, and its higher-level functions (ALL-MATCHES,
REGEX-REPLACE-ALL, SPLIT) pass a pre-compiled scanner CLOSURE to SCAN, which
then falls to the (t …) method and tries to parse the closure as a regex ->
PPCRE-SYNTAX-ERROR.

FIX
---
Make the type system recognise a closure as FUNCTION consistently — either in
the kernel (closure class a subclass of FUNCTION for TYPEP) or, like the
%MD-ARRAY string fix, in %TYPEP-BUILTIN so both TYPEP and CLOS dispatch agree.

Discovered 2026-07-24 unblocking bug-cl-ppcre after the SETF-place fixes.

====================================================================
RESOLUTION (v1.9.6, 2026-07-24)
%TYPEP-BUILTIN now returns T for (FUNCTION-object, 'FUNCTION) via FUNCTIONP.
Since both TYPEP and the CLOS applicability tests (%APPLICABLE-P,
%SPECIALIZER-POSITION) route through %TYPEP-BUILTIN, a method specialised on
((x function)) is now applicable to a closure, consistently with FUNCTIONP.
Covered by "closure is a function" in tests/test_conformance.c; suite green.

This was the last blocker for bug-cl-ppcre: with it, cl-ppcre LOADS and its
functions (SCAN, REGEX-REPLACE-ALL, ALL-MATCHES-AS-STRINGS, SPLIT, …) all work.
